    "result_text": "The full test suite passes: **56 tests run, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `timezone` field derived from the owner's locality/region using the fixed region-to-timezone table.\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a `timezone` property to the Owner schema (read-only IANA name string), documenting the `NSW\u2192Australia/Sydney, VIC\u2192Australia/Melbourne, QLD\u2192Australia/Brisbane` mapping.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/Localities.java`** \u2014 Added a `REGION_TIMEZONE` table plus two helpers: `timezoneForRegion(region)` (returns the IANA name or `null` for unknown/`UNKNOWN` regions) and `timezoneForOwner(owner)` (derives the region via the existing `forOwner` logic, then maps it to a timezone).\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added a MapStruct `@Mapping` for `timezone` that calls `Localities.timezoneForOwner(owner)`, mirroring the existing `locality` mapping.\n\nThe timezone is derived from the same region resolution used for `locality` (customerCode prefix \u2192 postcode range \u2192 city table), so the two fields stay consistent. Unknown regions yield a `null`/absent timezone, matching the pinned reference table in the tests.",
